#| Lines beginning "#|" are stripped by render.sh.
#|
#| `Copyright` is deliberately absent. The repository's LICENSE is the plain Apache-2.0 text
#| and its APPENDIX is unfilled — no file in this project asserts a copyright holder, so a
#| manifest submitted to someone else's repository must not be the first thing that does.
#| The field is optional (doc/manifest/schema/1.12.0/defaultLocale.md).
#|
#| `Icons` is absent because winget-pkgs populates it in its own validation pipeline; it is
#| not authorable in a pull request.
#|
#| FIELD ORDER follows the JSON schema's own property order, which is also the order komac
#| serialises in (winget-types, src/manifests/locale/mod.rs): ... Moniker, Tags, Agreements,
#| ReleaseNotes, ReleaseNotesUrl, PurchaseUrl, InstallationNotes, Documentations, Icons,
#| ManifestType, ManifestVersion. Note that this DISAGREES with the YAML sketch at the top of
#| doc/manifest/schema/1.12.0/defaultLocale.md, which lists Documentations before
#| ReleaseNotes; the schema and the tooling agree with each other and the sketch is the
#| outlier, so we follow the schema. This file used to put Documentations before
#| InstallationNotes. Nothing rejects either order — the point is that any tool which ever
#| rewrites this manifest will emit the schema order, and a gratuitous reordering diff on a
#| later version is a question we would rather not be asked.
#|
#| Every github.com URL below is pinned to the RELEASE TAG, not to `main`. winget-pkgs asks
#| for this explicitly for the licence — "If this is a link to the license file for an open
#| source project, it should be specific to the version for the package" — and the same
#| argument holds for the documents: a manifest for 0.1.1 that links `blob/main/` describes
#| whatever those files say years later, and 404s outright if one is renamed. A merged
#| manifest is never revised, so the URL has to be one that keeps meaning what it meant.
#| `PublisherUrl`, `PublisherSupportUrl` and `PackageUrl` stay unpinned on purpose: they name
#| the account, the issue tracker and the project, none of which is version-specific.
#|
#| `ReleaseNotes` is absent — render.sh has no input for it and inventing prose per release
#| is the kind of step nobody remembers. `ReleaseNotesUrl` carries the same information and
#| cannot go stale. If winget-releaser ever drives this package, komac fills `ReleaseNotes`
#| in from the GitHub release body by itself.
#|
#| `PrivacyUrl` is absent because there is no privacy policy to point at, which is the
#| honest answer for software that makes no network call of its own.
#|
#| `InstallationNotes` is what winget prints AFTER the install finishes, so it is written for
#| somebody who has just installed the thing and wants to use it — three commands, and the
#| one prerequisite that is not ours to install. Two things it used to say are gone. It said
#| "so the `boks` alias is on PATH": on 2026-08-16 winget created no alias, because symlink
#| creation was refused without Developer Mode and winget fell back to putting the package
#| directory on PATH, so the sentence named a mechanism that had not happened. And it carried
#| a paragraph explaining that a winget install does not raise SmartScreen — true, and
#| measured, but it is an explanation of something that did NOT occur, addressed to a reader
#| of the manifest rather than to the person who just installed Boks. docs/install.md is
#| where that belongs and is where it still is.
# Created by packaging/winget/render.sh in github.com/dagsommer/boks
# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://aka.ms/winget-manifest.defaultLocale.1.12.0.schema.json

PackageIdentifier: dagsommer.boks
PackageVersion: {{VERSION}}
PackageLocale: en-US
Publisher: Dag Sommer
PublisherUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer
PublisherSupportUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer/boks/issues
Author: Dag Sommer
PackageName: Boks
PackageUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer/boks
License: Apache-2.0
LicenseUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer/boks/blob/v{{VERSION}}/LICENSE
ShortDescription: Run coding agents and other untrusted developer tooling in local microVMs
Description: >-
  Boks runs coding agents and other untrusted developer tooling inside isolated microVMs on
  your own machine. It is a local-first, open-source alternative to Docker Sandboxes — no
  account, no cloud service, no telemetry.

  Each sandbox is a real virtual machine with its own kernel, not a container sharing yours,
  and every network flow out of it is judged against a policy you set before it is dialled.
  Your workspace is shared into the guest at its exact host path; nothing else is.

  On Windows the microVM runs on the Windows Hypervisor Platform. This is not WSL2 and needs
  no elevated terminal. Boks is experimental: read the project's verification record for what
  has been measured rather than what the feature list implies.
Moniker: boks
Tags:
- agent
- cli
- containerd
- developer-tools
- hypervisor
- isolation
- microvm
- sandbox
- security
- virtualization
ReleaseNotesUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer/boks/releases/tag/v{{VERSION}}
InstallationNotes: |-
  Open a new terminal so that boks is on PATH, then:

    boks doctor          check every prerequisite and print a remedy for each gap
    boks daemon start    start the containerd this package ships, configured for Boks
    boks run shell .     run a sandbox over the current directory

  Boks needs the Windows Hypervisor Platform. It is not code-signed; see docs/install.md.
Documentations:
- DocumentLabel: Installing Boks
  DocumentUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer/boks/blob/v{{VERSION}}/docs/install.md
- DocumentLabel: What has actually been verified
  DocumentUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer/boks/blob/v{{VERSION}}/docs/verification.md
- DocumentLabel: Boks on Windows
  DocumentUrl: https://github.com/dagsommer/boks/blob/v{{VERSION}}/docs/windows.md
ManifestType: defaultLocale
ManifestVersion: 1.12.0
